+\section{V2 Testing and Resonant-Tank Changes}
+\label{sec:res-v2-testing}
+
+The V2 prototype was first tested with its original resonant branch.
+Substantial heating was observed in the external series resonant inductor,
+described in the bench notes as approximately \SI{9.4}{\micro\henry}.
+The tank was then changed to use transformer leakage as its series
+inductance. The external inductor was removed, and the total resonant
+capacitance was changed to approximately \SI{1.66}{\nano\farad}.
+The prototype operated with this arrangement. The change removed the
+additional inductor from the current path; the overall loss reduction has
+not yet been quantified from matched input and output measurements.
+
+The supplied leakage measurements in
+Table~\ref{tab:mag-inherited-measurements} give an ideal series resonance
+near \SI{0.96}{\mega\hertz} with this capacitance. This is a calculation
+using the supplied component values, rather than a measurement of the
+installed V2 tank. The fitted transformer and loaded switching behavior
+still need to be recorded with the test configuration.
+
+\subsection{Half-Bridge Heating and Dead Time}
+
+The software dead-time setting was reported as approximately
+\SI{60}{\nano\second}. Lower half-bridge heating was observed after the
+tank and timing changes. ZVS was inferred in the bench notes from this
+improvement, but temperature alone does not establish the turn-on condition.
+A synchronized switch-voltage and current capture is still needed to
+verify ZVS. The firmware version, programmed timing readback, and effective
+dead time at the switches were not recorded with these observations.
+The nominal timing of the frozen controller source is described separately
+in Section~\ref{sec:hw-v2}.
+
+\subsection{Input-Bridge Heating}
+
+Heating remained around the input full-bridge rectifier, D4, D5, D9, and
+D10. The rectifier diodes were replaced, but the heating was not resolved.
+The devices were described in the bench notes as SiC rectifiers; the
+original and replacement part numbers still need to be recorded.
+
+An earlier load test was reported at \SI{240}{\volt} AC,
+\SI{50}{\hertz}, with a \SI{100}{\watt} constant-power load setting.
+Output voltage was reported to remain steady while bridge temperature
+continued to rise. The surface temperature was reported to reach about
+\SI{80}{\celsius} within a few seconds, then rise more slowly toward
+\SI{90}{\celsius}, when the test was stopped. The load setting is not a
+matched measurement of delivered power or a continuous rating.
+
+\begin{figure}[htbp]
+\centering
+\includegraphics[width=0.80\linewidth]{Data/measurements/v2_results/evidence/v2_bridge_thermal_original.jpg}
+\caption{Earlier V2 thermal image supplied with the load-test observations. The camera reports a regional surface maximum of \SI{70.0}{\celsius} in R3 and \SI{61.5}{\celsius} in R1. These are displayed surface-region values, not diode-junction temperatures. The image was not mapped to individual diodes or to a specific stage of the later diode-replacement and timing changes.}
+\label{fig:v2-bridge-thermal-observation}
+\end{figure}
+\FloatBarrier
+
+Limited trace and copper-pour area around the input bridge was identified
+during the layout review as a likely contributor. Narrow current paths can
+add resistive loss, and limited copper also restricts heat spreading from
+the diode pads. This explanation has not yet been isolated by current,
+voltage-drop, or thermal measurements. The unsuccessful diode replacement
+shows that the heating was not resolved by that component change alone.
+
+\subsection{Next Layout Changes and Test Status}
+
+Wider current paths and more copper for heat spreading are planned for the
+next layout. Moving the rectifier diodes to another side of the board is
+also being considered. The thermal effect of these changes still needs to
+be tested under matched conditions.
+
+V2 is operational, and the leakage-only tank has been exercised. The
+remaining issue is sustained input-bridge heating. A timed thermal run,
+matched input and output power, and switching captures are needed before
+the loss reduction, ZVS behavior, or continuous output can be confirmed.
+The bench observations and their later timing correction are retained in
+\sourcefile{Data/project_records/V2_Testing_Update.txt}; the original
+thermal image and its provenance are retained with the V2 evidence files.
