+V2 testing update: user-provided bench observations
+=================================================
+
+This record preserves the source wording used for the preliminary V2 testing
+section. It is a bench narrative, not a calibrated measurement table.
+
+Original dictation, verbatim:
+
+This was section for the version 2 testing. Version 2 testing we have tested in original state. There are still excessive losses on the secondary at where the series inductor, sorry, the 9.4 uH inductor. As a result, I have modified the resonant frequency and resonant tank to allow usage of just the pure leakage inductance from the transformer. I had to change to a 1.66 nanofarad resonant cap, but now I am able to successfully remove the series inductor, which reduces a bunch of losses. However, there is one problem in which the full bridge rectifier diodes, my silicon carbide ultra-fast recovery diodes, are having excessive heating. We'll check in on why it's the case. I believe it could be a routing error due to... sorry, I found that it's probably due to a routing error by me. I did not properly put enough... I didn't put enough copper for the traces, so as a result during the load testing it heats up significantly. Next versions I will increase the copper pour density, and hopefully that will also solve that problem as well. I have swapped out the rectifier diodes. As in addition, the problem is still have not fixed the issue of the heating. I'm going to see if I can move the diodes to a different side and hopefully reduce the overall thermal heating. Other than that, the V2 is up and running. Just the problem right now is our full bridge is losing a significant amount of heat. I had to adjust dead time to about 16 nanoseconds. My half bridge is achieving ZVS switching due to it no longer heating up like crazy anymore. But yep, systems for V2 are all good to go.
